outube  and like came into the office that was  like wearing on me because i could only  do that for so long  so i think that when push came to shove  it was just like this seems like a right  time  to pursue this and it's great because  like there's videos that kind of like  chronic all these moments  like oh i remember i was working on this  specific life hack video  um and i remember i had a call with jack  conte the ceo of patreon  where i just like it was more like a  personal call where it was like  hey i am  feeling like i'm feeling like between  i'm at this inflection point where  i feel like i have this opportunity with  youtube  but i don't really know if it's the  right thing  to like take the leap right and what he  told me and i want to give him full  credit for this was like  as you know this the boss at patreon  like as a ceo i do not want you to leave  but i will say like as a creator you'll  kick yourself if you don't  take this opportunity and from that  point it was like  i feel like i got just enough of a push  to where that  i was like okay i'm going to start  phasing myself out i'm going to tell  everybody that this is what my plan was  and it started  with a um it started very much like  putting my toes in the water where  i was potentially going to work  part-time or i was potentially going to  do some other sort of configuration  right  at patreon staying yeah while sticking  around  and at a certain point i that was  helpful because it kind of got the foot  in the door  but at a certain point i was like i need  a clean break i need  like i feel so overwhelmed with all  these things right now  i don't think i can like really juggle  at all  so i need to um i need to like break  away  and february was actually my last day  at patreon like february 3rd or 4th or  something like that  was my last day full time but i had a  few  months where i was like i'm planning to  leave so let's like  start let's hire my replacement let's  finish up these projects or whatever  uh and so i spent those months like  wrapping things up so it was a very long  phase out um but that was just because i  cared about  the people around me and i cared about  like not leaving things in like a super  a super twisted or like complicated  situation i wanted it to be as clean a  break as possible  yeah i feel like one of the things that  as i've like talked to more and more  people who've made a jump from  their tech career into maybe content  creation or something else is there is  there's this kind of like stars align  moment where it's like  it's not like the stars aren't ever  gonna align again but it's like  the distance between jumping from this  platform to this platform is as short as  it's ever been  and while it's not it's still not easy  to make the jump it's  like an opportunity to do so and so like  everyone makes a decision on whether to  do that at the time or not  and the other thing that i think like is  really interesting that like i think  your story was the first thing that  really made me  think this way and i've seen it since  too of just like  some people when they think about like  leaving their tech career they're just  like like they  imagine it to be just like i'm done i  quit  hand on my notice today and that's it  and there are some people who do that  but like more often than not  i found that it's a lot about just like  literally having conversations with your  manager  like making sure that you are phasing  yourself out and like  really kind of taking slow incremental  steps to get to a point where  you feel okay about leaving like it's  both kind of like a  journey not just for taking care of the  people who you're working with and the  code but it's also kind of like a slow  emotional journey for yourself too  because yeah like when i joined netflix  i told my manager i was like i don't  know if this is what i want to do but  i'm here to explore it and she was like  yeah explore and so i spent the whole  year being like  um i think this is still what i want to  do and so by the time that i jumped she  was just like yeah this is kind of a  no-brainer like you've told me this  before and it makes sense  so i will just say that like everybody's  situation is different  i decided that i wanted to stick around  and like wrap up all the loose ends  you you'd never owe that to a company  you know what i mean  like like if it's the right thing for  you it's the right thing for you and no  one is going to like hold that against  you  i think for me it was more it was less  about the company itself and more about  the individual relationships with the  people  who i was working with i was like  working on a project with tony over here  and i was like okay tony like let's  finish this thing up by tax day and then  i was like over here like  working with my engineers and i was like  let's get you guys into a good place and  i had my pm  you know and it's like it was it was  really just about  like everyone's capacity and intolerance  for this is different but i  knew that i stayed way longer than i  needed to  you know what i mean like i and that was  my own decision and  if if if it was not my own decision then  i would have made  i would have sort of challenged it right  if the company was like  can you stay even longer or can you you  know it's like if there was any sort of  challenge to my own decision i wanted to  make sure it worked for me  first before i put the company's you  know needs above my own because  there could be various livelihood mental  health or whatever concerns  around like a job and if you're in the  position where  you can afford like to like  up and leave your post like that is  itself a privilege and so like if you  find yourself in a similar position to  me just make sure you're being selfish  and like  prioritizing yourself because a thing  that i say and i don't think you can  really like regardless of who you are  you can't really argue with